course: everyday
lesson: 254
title: Classroom rules and homework
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A classroom is shared by many people, so its rules are about respect: ask before you borrow, keep your space tidy, do your class job well and use school devices only as allowed.
explain: When there is a problem, a calm word works best: ask a noisy classmate kindly to be quiet, and if you were blamed for something you did not do, explain politely later, in private.
explain: Be patient with a classmate who is still learning English: speak slowly, use gestures and pictures, and each week they will understand more.
explain: Homework is yours: write in your own words and say where you found facts, because copying someone else's text is plagiarism, a kind of cheating.
explain: A girl in Dublin has been doing homework for two hours every night; her mum talks to the teacher, who explains it was meant to take only thirty minutes and changes it.
fact: Non-uniform or dress-up days usually raise money for charity, so you check the theme and bring the donation if one is asked.
fact: You ask before borrowing a classmate's pencil or rubber and give it back when you finish.
fact: At the end of the lesson you put books, pencils and papers back where they belong, so things are quick to find and nobody trips over them.
fact: If a classmate's noise stops you working, you ask kindly first and tell the teacher if it continues.
fact: If you are told off for something you did not do, you stay calm and explain politely later, in private if possible, because a calm talk is more likely to be heard.
fact: A class job, like handing out books, is done carefully and on time, because the whole class waits on it.
fact: With a classmate who speaks little English, you speak slowly, use gestures and pictures, and are patient.
fact: School computers and tablets are used only for what the teacher allows, without changing settings or opening other sites.
fact: Filters on school devices are there to keep children safe online.
fact: Copying text and passing it off as your own is plagiarism, a kind of cheating.
fact: In homework you write in your own words and say where you found the facts.
fact: Homework practises what you learnt; if you keep skipping it, you fall behind and it gets harder to catch up.
fact: If you are too tired to finish homework, you stop, tell a parent, and send the teacher a note.
fact: If homework always takes far too long, a parent can talk to the teacher, who may adjust it.
fact: A lost worksheet can be got again from a classmate, the teacher or the school website.
exercise: It is a dress-up day for charity. What do you check? | The theme and whether a donation is asked | Who has the most expensive costume | Whether dress-up days mean no lessons | Nothing, you just come in uniform
exercise: You need a rubber and your friend has one. What do you do? | Ask first and give it back after | Take it quietly while she is not looking at you | Keep it until next week, then return it | Take it and say nothing, it's only a rubber
exercise: The lesson ends. What do you do with your books and pencils? | Put them back where they belong | Leave them spread out on the table for the next class | Put them on the floor under your chair | Push them onto your neighbour's desk
exercise: The boy next to you keeps tapping and humming, and you cannot work. What first? | Ask him kindly to be quiet | Shout across the room at him to stop it | Throw his pencil away so he cannot tap | Tap and hum louder back at him
exercise: You asked kindly, but the noise goes on. What next? | Tell the teacher | Start a fight with him | Leave the classroom without asking | Make even more noise
exercise: The teacher tells the whole class off for a mess you did not make. What do you do? | Stay calm and explain politely later | Argue loudly in front of everyone at once | Slam your books down on the desk | Refuse to do any work all day
exercise: You are given the job of handing out the books. How do you do it? | Carefully and on time | Only when you feel like doing it | Throw them across the room | Give them only to your best friends
exercise: A new girl from Brazil speaks little English. How can you help? | Speak slowly and use gestures and pictures | Speak louder and faster so she has to keep up | Ignore her until she learns English | Laugh kindly when she makes mistakes
exercise: You are on the class tablet for a maths game. What may you do? | Only what the teacher allowed | Change the settings and the background picture | Open video sites when nobody looks | Download a new game
exercise: Why do school tablets have filters? | To keep children safe online | To make the school internet run much faster | To stop the battery running out | To hide the maths games from the children
exercise: You find a great paragraph online for your project. What do you do? | Put it in your own words and say where you found it | Copy it word for word and say you wrote it yourself | Copy it but change one or two words | Copy it without saying anything
exercise: What is plagiarism? | Passing off someone else's words as your own | Writing a project in your own words | Reading a book in the school library | Asking a teacher to help you with spelling
exercise: You are too exhausted to finish your homework. What do you do? | Stop, tell a parent and send the teacher a note | Stay up until midnight however tired you are | Copy a friend's homework in the morning | Throw the homework away
exercise: Your homework takes three hours every single night. What should happen? | Tell a parent so they can talk to the teacher | Keep quiet and stay up later every night | Stop doing homework at all | Ask a friend to do half of it for you every evening
exercise: You lost your worksheet. What do you do? | Ask the teacher or a classmate, or check the website | Do nothing and hope the teacher forgets about it | Say the dog ate it | Make up answers on blank paper
test: A boy in Sydney borrowed his friend's pencil last week and still has it. What should he have done? | Given it back when he finished | Kept it, since it is only a pencil and pencils are cheap | Swapped it for a worse one | Lent it to someone else in the class
test: Why is a tidy desk useful at the end of the day? | You find things fast and the room stays safe | It makes the teacher give you a sticker every day | It lets you go home earlier than the other classes | Tidy desks never need cleaning or dusting again
test: A girl copies a whole page from a website for her project on whales. What is wrong with this? | It is plagiarism, a kind of cheating | The website will be closed down because of her | Whales do not like it | Nothing, everything on the internet is free to copy
test: Why is it better to explain in private when you were wrongly blamed? | A calm talk is more likely to be heard | Teachers never listen to anything said in class | It lets you skip the lesson | It makes the others get punished instead of you
test: Two months after arriving, a boy from Poland understands much more English. What helped most? | Classmates who were patient and spoke slowly | Classmates who laughed whenever he got a word wrong | Everyone speaking faster | Nobody talking to him so he had to listen
test: A boy changes the background and settings on the shared class laptop. Why is this wrong? | School devices are shared and used only as allowed | It makes the laptop much faster for the next person | Settings can never be changed back | Laptops do not have settings
test: A girl falls asleep over her homework at 9 p.m. What is the sensible choice? | Stop, tell a parent, and send the teacher a note | Drink coffee and keep going until it is all done | Copy the answers from a friend | Hide the unfinished sheet in her bag
test: The classroom helper forgets to hand out the reading books, and the lesson starts late. What does this show? | Class jobs help the whole class when done on time | Class jobs do not matter | The teacher should do every job | Books are not really needed in a reading lesson
test: A boy cannot find his worksheet, which is due tomorrow. What is a good idea? | Check the school website or ask the teacher for a copy | Say he never got one, so he does not have to do it | Skip the homework without telling anyone | Copy a friend's finished sheet
test: got told off for talking but it wasn't me! gonna shout | Stay calm and explain politely later | Shout out loud that it wasn't you, right now | Blame the person next to you | Sulk for the rest of the day
test: copied a bit from a website for homework, that's fine right? | No, use your own words and say where facts came from | Yes, everything on the internet is free to copy | Yes, if you change one word | Only if the teacher doesn't check it with a computer
test: lost my worksheet, homework's due tmrw | Get it again from a classmate, the teacher or the website | Don't do the homework and hope nobody asks for it | Say the dog ate it | Make up your own worksheet
test: new boy hardly speaks english, how do i explain the game | Speak slowly, use gestures and pictures | Shout really loud so he understands you | Talk very fast | Don't bother, he won't get it
explain: A boy in Michigan kept leaning back on two legs of his chair; it slid out and he cracked the back of his head on the floor and had to go home with a concussion (a brain injury from a knock to the head).
explain: A girl in Melbourne threw a pencil to a friend across the room; the point hit another boy in the eye and he needed treatment at the eye hospital.
fact: Tipping back on chairs is a common cause of head injuries in classrooms.
fact: Sharp things thrown in class, even in fun, can hurt an eye for life.
test: rocking back on my chair, it's fine i'm good at it | Sit with all four legs down, it can slide | Fine as long as you hold on to the desk with one hand | Fine if the floor is carpet | Only while the teacher's not looking
test: Why is throwing a pencil across the classroom a bad idea? | Its point can hit someone in the eye | It might break the pencil lead inside | It wastes time | Pencils are school property and cost the school money
test: never did homework all term, who cares | You fall behind and it gets harder to catch up | Nobody notices if you just sit quietly | It doesn't count | You'll learn it all next year anyway
test: did u hear a kid was rocking on his chair and went straight over and cracked his head on the desk? real lesson? | Don't tip back on chairs; it causes head injuries | Rock only on the back two legs, never on one | Only plastic chairs tip over | Lean on the wall behind you, then rocking is totally safe
test: apparently some kid flicked a pencil at his mate and it went in his eye and he's blind now. what's the true lesson? | Never throw sharp things in class, even in fun | Throw only blunt pencils | Wear glasses in class so it doesn't matter if it hits | Eyes heal quickly from anything that hits them
test: everyone's saying the new boy who speaks little English is dumb. pass it on? | No, he's learning English; be patient and kind | Yes, he doesn't understand anything | Yes, if it's true | Yes, so the teacher moves him into a different class
exam: Identify the purpose of filters upon school computers and tablets. | To keep children safe online | To make the devices slower | To prevent homework being saved upon the school system | To stop teachers using the internet
exam: Which of the following constitutes appropriate conduct where a pupil is reprimanded for something he did not do? | He remains calm and explains politely later | He shouts that the teacher is wrong | He blames another pupil loudly | He refuses to work for the remainder of the day
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of homework. | It is written in one's own words, with sources stated | It is copied from websites without any acknowledgement | It is completed by an older sibling | It is copied from a classmate's book
exam: Ascertain the proper manner of communicating with a classmate who speaks little English. | Speaking slowly with gestures and pictures, patiently | Shouting the words more loudly and repeating them | Ignoring him until he learns | Speaking only through other children
